@HaiShaw could you add the `run-ci` label here when you get a chance?

The gate currently exits with `Missing required label 'run-ci'`, so every test job is skipped and this PR has never actually been exercised by CI — only `lint` and the other label-independent checks have run.

For context: this is a correctness fix in the fused DSA indexer top-k, found while profiling our own GLM-5.2 decode runs on MI355X. When a threshold coarse bin holds more than `kMaxNumTie` (2048) candidates, the collect pass keeps whichever arrived first and the kernel silently returns a wrong top-k set; device-side counters put that on ~0.04% of rows on a real serving run, with the largest observed bin at 4139. #35257 and #36807 report related problems in this area. #37942 has been folded into this PR and closed, so this is now the only one to look at.

@HaiShaw thanks for the earlier approval. Could you help nudge this one along, or advise on how to proceed?

@DarkSharpness requested changes on Sep 9 with three performance comments. All three are addressed in `9a57b69a20` and measured on MI355X — the hot path is unchanged and the refinement path drops 29–35%. I replied with the numbers on Sep 10 and again on Sep 11 after a rebase, but there has been no response in four days, and the `CHANGES_REQUESTED` is still recorded against the old commit `7a83d7fdd5`.

Every CI job that exercises this kernel is now green on both vendors, and `mergeable` is `true` again. Happy to keep waiting if a re-review is coming — just flagging that the requested work is done.

@DarkSharpness ready for another look when you have time.

One clarification on the hold: #38798 itself has not merged, but the top-k restructure you asked us to wait for landed separately as #39648, so we rebased onto that — the port is `e503afa6bd`. Happy to keep holding if you would rather we wait for the rest of #38798 too.

#39648 left all four truncation sites unchanged, so the defect is as it was. Your three points still hold: no early-out scan, at most four input passes, no separate staging pass. CI is green on every job that exercises this kernel, B200 and H100 included, and 304 pass locally on MI355X with the hot path unchanged. Numbers and the remaining CUDA-register gap are in the description.

@DarkSharpness applied your patch as `a2f2c8c445`; test updates are in `99e0d96d30`.

On MI355X, 300 tests pass. Five paired runs on one GPU show the hot path at −2.0% / −0.7% versus main; `narrow_bin` is 2.6–5.3% faster than the previous refinement. A clean local JIT build is 9.24 s versus 11.28 s before your patch (main: 8.86 s). gfx950 is 71 VGPRs, zero spills, LDS unchanged.

I cannot measure B200 locally, so a rerun of your CUDA benchmark would be appreciated.

@DarkSharpness thanks for the approval. Merging current `main` needed two ROCm-only follow-ups for the long-row split path from #39931, both in `dc895454f4`:

- Compile fix: the split path still called `coarse_bin_lower_bound`, which this PR removes, so ROCm topk_v2 would fail to build after merge. It now uses `coarse_bin_bounds`.
- The split path carried its own truncating tie copy, so an overflowing bin was still inexact there. Blocks now publish the uncapped bin count and the last block runs `refine_ties` on overflow. Without overflow the split path is unchanged (same timings for batch 1-8, 64K-128K).

All 324 `test_topk_v2.py` cases pass on MI355X, including a new split-routed overflow shape that fails without the fix. CUDA code is unchanged.

@DarkSharpness @HaiShaw once CI finishes, could one of you merge this, and rerun any unrelated failures? Thank you.
